  "user_scenario": "Dorothy Ellsworth is seventy-three and lives on her own in a first-floor flat on Larkspur Avenue in Alder. She keeps her post in a wire basket by the door and opens it on Sunday evenings, which is how she came across the letter from Ridgecrest saying that her prescription drug plan would be stopped if the money owing on it was not paid. It has sat on her mind all week, because two of her tablets are ones she cannot do without, and she telephones on the Monday morning with the letter flattened out in front of her.\n\nShe opens by saying she has had a letter about her drug plan being cancelled and that she wants to pay whatever is owed today and be done with it, and that she would rather do it over the telephone now than put a cheque in the post, which she does not trust to be quick enough. She does not know what the figure is: the letter talks about an amount, she says, but she cannot make out which of the numbers on it is the one she is meant to pay, and if the agent asks her what she thinks she owes she says she truly does not know and would rather be told than guess.\n\nShe gives her name as Dorothy Ellsworth when it is asked for, and her date of birth as 1953-04-18. If the agent wants the number from her Medicare card she reads it out as 2EQ7FK4WD62, spelling the letters slowly. Her telephone number, if that is wanted instead, is 3125540198, and she can give her address as 417 Larkspur Avenue, Apartment 6B, Alder. She is content for the agent to look over everything on her record and says so plainly; nobody is appointed to act for her and no-one else is on the line with her.\n\nShe will not touch her bank details until she has been told a figure. Once the agent tells her what the settlement comes to she accepts it without argument, being of a generation that does not haggle with an insurance company, and asks only whether that finishes the matter. When the agent hands the payment over for her to make herself, she makes it, and reads the plan's payment reference back if she is asked to confirm it. If the agent names a figure and then goes quiet, she waits a moment and asks whether there is something she has to do at her end.\n\nIf she is asked whether she has any other cover with Ridgecrest she says she does not think anything else of hers is running at the moment, and if she is pressed on whether she ever had anything else she says the agent had better look, because her memory for that sort of thing is poor. She cannot say when she last paid or how much: the money goes out by cheque, she says, and the agent should read it off the account rather than take her word for it.\n\nOnce she hears that the money has been taken and that the drug plan is not going to be stopped, she thanks the agent, says that is a weight off, and rings off.",
